<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of the Netherlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Netherlands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of the Netherlands is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> monarchy established in 1815 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, succeeding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. It governed the later phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The name Netherlands is also used on the site for the Dutch lands more generally, including their trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Netherlands" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Aceh War against the <b>Netherlands</b> from 1873, Acehnese fighters defended fortified villages and later waged a long guerrilla war under leaders such as Teungku Chik di Tiro, Teuku Umar and Cut Nyak Dhien, inflicting heavy losses on the Dutch colonial army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall, 30,000 wealthy Armenians moved to Lusignan Cyprus and merchant families founded or joined diaspora communities in France, Italy, the <b>Netherlands</b>, Poland and Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the French Revolutionary Wars, after the <b>Netherlands</b> had become the French-allied Batavian Republic, Britain sent a fleet under Admiral George Elphinstone and troops under General James Craig to keep the sea route to India out of French hands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Burgundian and Habsburg rule the county kept its own institutions, its sovereign court at Mons and its customary law, codified in the general charters of 1619, while its rulers governed the <b>Netherlands</b> as a whole through governors and councils in Brussels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From the accession of Charles I in 1516, Castile was the heartland of a Habsburg monarchy that spanned the <b>Netherlands</b>, Italy and the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Norway was a land of freehold and tenant farmers, fishermen and foresters, whose timber, sawn in water-powered mills, was exported in large quantities to the <b>Netherlands</b> and Britain, and whose dried and salted fish from the north was traded through Bergen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
